 — gravity field coefficients, static; gfct — time-variable, time series of coefficients) has no epoch. Its pad is 0.0 which is indistinguishable from a real epoch of J2000.0.\n\nProposal: presence bit 32 in existing presence mask: absent = bit clear, present = bit set.\n\nIs this honest? Issues:\n\n1. The strongest confound: the presence bit tells you \"the epoch field is present/valid\", but it conflates two different reasons for absence:\n   - Structural absence: the record type (static gfc) has no epoch field at all — the concept doesn't apply.\n   - Value absence: a time-variable record (gfct) with an unknown/uncomputed/unmappable epoch — the concept applies but the value is missing.\n   Both would render as bit clear. Downstream consumers can't distinguish \"not applicable\" from \"not available\", and a linter/validator can't tell a bug (epoch lost, zero-filled) from correct static data. Actually more precisely: zero-fill of a missing epoch in a gfct record produces exactly the same bit pattern as a legitimate static record if the writer forgets to set the bit. The failure mode is silent and lands on a legal value (0.0 = J2000.0).\n\nActually, the deeper rift: the pad value 0.0 is inside the value domain. So a writer that fails to set the presence bit produces a record that is *syntactically valid and semantically wrong* — epoch J2000.0 for a static source. The presence bit is an \"out-of-band\" annotation of an \nScroll to latest message\nMessage\nMiMo V2.6 Pro\nSmall model\n·\nLinkedIn\n·\nYouTube\n·\nSubstack\n·\nPrivacy\n·\nTerms\n·\nAPI"